---
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import { loadDataset } from "../lib/data";
import { SUM_TOLERANCE } from "../lib/schema";

const { predictions, meta, isMock } = loadDataset();
const pipe = meta.pipeline;
const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB");
const fieldSizes = [...new Set(predictions.map((p) => p.drivers.length))].sort((a, b) => a - b);

const contents = [
  { id: "short", label: "The short version" },
  { id: "model", label: "The model" },
  { id: "data", label: "The data" },
  { id: "snapshots", label: "Three predictions" },
  { id: "predicted", label: "What is predicted" },
  { id: "scoring", label: "Scoring" },
  { id: "rejected", label: "Tried and rejected" },
  { id: "evaluation", label: "Evaluation" },
  { id: "bugs", label: "Two scorer bugs" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What it cannot see" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="What Sector 4 predicts, which data it is built on, how each prediction is scored against the result, and what the model cannot see."
  image="/og/methodology.png"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology/" }]}
>
  <section class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the numbers are made.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A prediction you cannot check is a claim, not a forecast. Everything below is here so the
      numbers on this site can be argued with.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="contents">
      {contents.map((c, i) => (
        <a class="btn btn-soft rise" style={`--i:${i}`} href={`#${c.id}`}>
          {c.label}
        </a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </section>

  <section id="short" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">The short version.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Three predictions per race.</strong> One before practice, using each driver's and
          team's recent form, the championship standings and recent qualifying. One after practice,
          adding this weekend's practice pace. One after qualifying, using where each driver
          qualified, their practice pace, their championship points and their share of their team's
          points.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Chances, not picks.</strong> A 30% chance to win means the driver would win about
          3 times if the race could be run 10 times. Favourites lose, and a good prediction still
          misses winners.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scored after every race.</strong> Each prediction gets a score based on the chance
          it gave the driver who won, where lower is better. Next to it is the score of a simple
          rule that knew the same things.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Honest about the limits.</strong> Tested on this season, none of the three has
          done clearly better than its simple rule: going by qualifying position after qualifying,
          and by the championship order before it. What the after-qualifying model does well is say
          how likely things are: when it says 20%, that happens about one time in five.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not betting advice.</strong> Nothing here has been tested against bookmakers'
          odds.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The rest of this page is the detail, for anyone who wants to check the working.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What the model is, and what it is not.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The model is a logistic regression, a standard statistical method that turns inputs into
        probabilities, with <strong>four inputs</strong>: where the driver qualified, their best
        practice lap relative to the fastest car, their championship points, and their share of
        their team's points. Qualifying position enters as a smooth curve rather than a straight
        line, because the gap between first and second is not the gap between fifteenth and
        sixteenth.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the after-qualifying model. The before-practice prediction has its own model, chosen
        separately: the same kind of logistic regression, with thirteen inputs that all exist before
        a weekend starts. They are each driver's and each team's recent finishes, points and
        retirements, their championship standings, and their average qualifying position over their
        last three races. It is a weaker model than the one after qualifying, because it cannot see
        the weekend.
      </p>
      <p>
        It replaced an earlier before-practice model that simply reused two of the after-qualifying
        inputs, championship points and share of team points. Tested properly, that earlier model
        did worse than the simple rule of championship order, both over 2019–2025 and this season.
        The new one beats the earlier model clearly and beat the rule over 2019–2025, but this
        season it has not done better than the rule. Predictions the earlier model made stay on the
        site as they were published, and each race page says which model made each prediction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The after-practice prediction uses the same thirteen inputs plus four from this weekend's
        practice before qualifying: best-lap pace, long-run pace, and how many laps were run. It was
        chosen the same way. Practice made it better than the same model without practice over
        2019–2025, and this season it has been ahead of both that model and the championship-order
        rule, though fourteen races are too few to rule out luck.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four inputs is not a simplification made for the sake of the explanation. Larger versions
        were built and measured — nineteen inputs, twenty-two, thirty-three — and every one of them
        scored worse. Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form were all tried,
        and all three made the predictions less accurate rather than more.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>The model has not been shown to beat a simple rule.</strong> The rule is
          qualifying order: the chance of winning that each qualifying position has had in past
          races, and nothing else. Across 2019–2025 the model scored better, but those are the races
          it was chosen on, so that comparison flatters it. The one clean test — the 2026 season,
          kept aside from every decision and scored once — was <strong>inconclusive</strong>. The
          model was slightly ahead on average, by less than fourteen races of a season under new
          rules can tell apart from luck.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        So this site does not claim that the model beats qualifying order. What it does claim is
        that the win probabilities are well calibrated — when it says 20%, that happens about one
        time in five — and <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a> prints the simple rule's
        score beside the model's on every race, so the question is yours to settle rather than ours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {isMock && (
    <section class="wrap band chapter">
      <h2 class="h3">What is running right now.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>The numbers on these pages are sample data, not the model's output.</strong> The
            model described above exists and has been measured, but this site is still showing
            probabilities generated to exercise the layout — a Plackett–Luce draw over a plausible
            field, scored against invented results. They say nothing about any real race.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Real predictions are being written one race at a time, each committed before the session
          it predicts and scored afterwards in a separate step, so the record cannot be edited once
          the result is known. This page will lose this notice when those reach the site. The banner
          at the top of every page and the label on every link preview come from the same flag, so
          neither can be forgotten.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section id="data" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">The data.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Results, qualifying, grids, laps, pit stops and status codes come from the Ergast schema:
        the Kaggle Formula 1 World Championship export for 1950 to 2024, and the Jolpica F1 API for
        2025 onward. Session telemetry, timing and weather from 2018 onward come from FastF1.
      </p>
      <p>Five rules govern that pipeline, and they are enforced rather than intended.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div class="rise" style="--i:0">
          <dt>Nothing is invented.</dt>
          <dd>
            A missing value stays missing and is flagged. No estimating it from its neighbours, no
            filling it in later, no plausible stand-ins.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:1">
          <dt>Every row says where it came from.</dt>
          <dd>
            Every record carries the source it came from, so any figure can be traced back to the
            file it was read from.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:2">
          <dt>Conflicts are logged, not resolved quietly.</dt>
          <dd>
            Where two sources disagree, both values are recorded with the evidence, and the
            resolution is a documented decision rather than a silent preference.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:3">
          <dt>One clock.</dt>
          <dd>
            Times are stored in UTC and shown in your own time zone by the page. The original local
            dates are kept as they were.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:4">
          <dt>Builds are repeatable.</dt>
          <dd>
            The same inputs always produce exactly the same outputs, which is what makes a change
            checkable at all.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      {pipe && (
        <>
          <h3 class="title sub-h">What that adds up to</h3>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="table">
              <caption class="sr-only">
                Row counts per table, split by the source each row came from.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Table</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Rows
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Kaggle
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Jolpica
                  </th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {Object.entries(pipe.tables).map(([name, t], i) => (
                  <tr class="rise" style={`--i:${i}`}>
                    <th scope="row">{name.replace(/_/g, " ")}</th>
                    <td class="n num">{fmt.format(t.rows)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.kaggle ?? 0)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.jolpica ?? 0)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <dl class="figures">
            <div>
              <dt>Seasons covered</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.seasons.first}–{pipe.seasons.last}
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Validation checks</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.validation.passed ?? "—"} passed, {pipe.validation.failed ?? "—"} failed
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Corrections applied</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.corrections.total}</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Unresolved conflicts</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.unresolved_conflicts}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Of those corrections,{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.official ?? 0}</span> cite an official
            source — an FIA classification document or a formula1.com results page — and{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.internal ?? 0}</span> cite an internal
            derivation, where the dataset contradicts itself and the resolution follows from other
            rows rather than from an outside document. Both kinds are recorded with what changed and
            why. Session timing from <span class="num">{pipe.enrichment_first_season}</span> onward
            is layered on top from FastF1, and is used to fit the model but never published — see{" "}
            <a href="/credits/">credits</a>.
          </p>
        </>
      )}
      <p>
        The current data version is <span class="num">{meta.data_version}</span>. Sources and
        licences are listed on <a href="/credits/">the credits page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="snapshots" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">Three predictions per race.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A prediction only means something if you know when it was made, so each race gets up to
        three, each with a deadline. Before practice is locked when the first session of the weekend
        starts; after practice when the first qualifying session starts, which is sprint qualifying
        on a sprint weekend; after qualifying when the race starts. A correction before the deadline
        is published as a new revision beside the original, and the race page shows both.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Before practice</dt>
          <dd>
            Published before any car runs, so it knows nothing from this weekend. It uses recent
            form, the championship standings and recent qualifying, all from earlier races. Compared
            with the simple rule of championship order.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>After practice</dt>
          <dd>
            Published after the last practice before qualifying: after FP3, or after FP1 on a sprint
            weekend. It adds this weekend's practice pace to the before-practice inputs, measured
            among the drivers who ran in FP2 or FP3 (FP1 on a sprint weekend). Compared with the
            simple rule of championship order, which did better than going by practice times.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>After qualifying</dt>
          <dd>
            Published after qualifying, once the qualifying order and this weekend's practice pace
            are known. It uses qualifying position rather than the starting grid, so penalties
            applied afterwards are not in it; <a href="#limits">why is below</a>. This is the
            sharpest of the three and the one a race page shows first. Compared with the simple rule
            of qualifying order.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        All of them remain on the race page afterwards. Comparing them shows how much each part of
        the weekend changed the picture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="predicted" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What is predicted.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>For every driver in the field, each prediction gives:</p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Win, podium, top ten</dt>
          <dd>
            The chance of finishing there or better. Only the win chance has been tested against
            past races; podium and top ten are worked out from it by simulating the race many
            thousands of times.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>DNF</dt>
          <dd>
            The chance of not being classified as a finisher. It comes from a separate model that
            has not been tested.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Average finish</dt>
          <dd>
            Where the driver finishes on average across all the simulated races, not a rounded guess
            at one place. The full spread is drawn on each race page.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Top factors</dt>
          <dd>
            The handful of inputs moving that driver's chance most, whether each helps or hurts, and
            by how much relative to the others. Weights only, never a lap time or any timing value.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Those numbers have to hold together, so the build checks them rather than trusting them.
        Across a field of {fieldSizes.join(" or ")} drivers, the win chances must add up to 100%,
        the podium chances to 300% because there are three places, and the top-ten chances to
        1,000%; each driver's chances of finishing in each position must add up to 100%; and no
        driver may be likelier to win than to finish on the podium, or likelier to finish on the
        podium than in the top ten. The only slack allowed is{" "}
        <span class="num">{SUM_TOLERANCE.toExponential(0)}</span>, for rounding in the arithmetic. A
        file that fails any of these stops the build instead of reaching a page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scoring" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">How a prediction is scored.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Scoring happens once, against the latest revision published before that prediction's
        deadline, and the result is never recomputed. A revision published after its deadline is
        listed on the race page and never scored. Four numbers per race:
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Score (log loss)</dt>
          <dd>
            Based only on the chance given to the driver who actually won:{" "}
            <span class="num">−ln p</span>, where <span class="num">p</span> is that chance. Giving
            the winner 100% scores 0; giving them 50% scores 0.69; giving them 5% scores 3.0. It
            punishes confident misses hard, which is the point. Lower is better.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Brier score</dt>
          <dd>
            Looks at every driver's chance, not just the winner's: how far each was from what
            happened, squared and added up, counting the winner as 100% and everyone else as 0%. It
            runs from 0 to 2, so one disastrous race cannot swamp a season the way it can with the
            score above. Lower is better.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Picked the winner</dt>
          <dd>
            Whether the driver given the best chance of winning won. Crude, and included because it
            is the number everyone actually checks.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Podium picks right</dt>
          <dd>How many of the three likeliest podium finishers made the podium, out of three.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        A driver who was predicted but did not start is left out, and everyone else's chances are
        scaled up to add back to 100%. The before-practice prediction copies its list of drivers
        from the previous race's starters, so a driver can also start without having been predicted.
        The race page says so whenever either happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the winner is one of those drivers, or was published with a win chance of 0.0% at six
        decimal places, the prediction gave the winner no chance at all, and there is no score to
        give: the formula above has no answer for zero. It is left empty rather than filled with a
        stand-in, which would measure the stand-in and not the prediction. Everything else still
        counts: the winner is missed, the Brier score charges the full miss, and the race counts as
        one where the model did not beat the simple rule. The track record's average leaves such a
        race out and says how many it left out.
      </p>
      <p>
        A score means nothing on its own, so <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a> prints
        two more: what picking a driver at random would have scored, which is the floor, and what a
        simple rule that knew the same things scored, which is the bar. That rule is qualifying
        order for the after-qualifying prediction and championship order for the before-practice
        one. Both are worked out the same way as the model's own score, on the same race, from data
        available before it started.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="rejected" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What was tried and rejected.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The four inputs are not a first guess that happened to work. Larger and cleverer models were
        built, measured against the same simple rule on the same races, and found worse.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Nine extra inputs that turned out to be noise</dt>
          <dd>
            Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form, nine inputs in three
            groups. None of them made a difference that could be told apart from chance, and adding
            them made the score worse: 1.3286 with form alone, 1.3480 once circuit history was
            added, 1.3765 once tyre data was.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A gradient-boosted tree model</dt>
          <dd>
            A more flexible kind of model. It memorised the races it learned from, scoring 0.0069 on
            those against 0.2155 on races it had not seen. Holding it back heavily closed that gap
            to 0.0256, and it still scored worse than the simple model race by race.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>An Elo rating</dt>
          <dd>
            A rating system like the one used in chess. It scored 2.0256 against the
            qualifying-order rule's 1.4340. A rating that sees only the driver cannot see the car,
            and in this sport the car is most of the answer.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A ranking model with simulated race orders</dt>
          <dd>
            It produced whole finishing orders that hang together, which separate win, podium and
            top-ten figures do not, but it lost to the simple rule by a margin too large to be
            chance. Its simulation still supplies the finishing-order charts on race pages.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="evaluation" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">How the model is evaluated.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each race is predicted by a model trained only on the races before it, never on the race
        itself or anything after. That is slow, because the model is retrained for every race, and
        it is the only way a comparison with a simple rule that works the same way means anything.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Uncertainty measured race by race</dt>
          <dd>
            The ranges of uncertainty are worked out by resampling whole races (a bootstrap), not
            individual drivers. Twenty drivers in one race are not twenty pieces of evidence: if the
            model misreads the car, it misreads all twenty at once.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Compared with the simple rule on the same races</dt>
          <dd>Race by race, so a run of unusually hard races cannot decide the comparison.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Honest chances, not just the right order</dt>
          <dd>
            Getting the order right is one test. Calibration is another: does a stated 20% happen
            about one time in five? This site publishes the chances themselves, so the second test
            is the one that matters, and a model with a better score but less honest chances would
            be the wrong choice here.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The evidence that the model beats the simple rule comes from{" "}
          <strong>the races it was chosen on</strong>. The comparison across 2019–2025 is what chose
          this model, so it flatters it. The one clean test — 2026, kept aside from every decision
          and scored once — was inconclusive. <a href="/track-record/">The track record</a> is the
          real ongoing test.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="bugs" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">Two bugs in the scorer, and how they were caught.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Both were found by the cheapest thing in the project, and both would have quietly flattered
        every number measured afterwards.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>An even guess picked 99.4% of winners</dt>
          <dd>
            The field arrives sorted by finishing position, so picking the highest chance fell back
            on row order and handed a tied predictor the winner every time. Breaking ties by driver
            id instead gave 19.9% — still wrong, because the lowest id in this era belongs to a
            driver who won a fifth of those races. Ties are now shared out evenly, and an even guess
            across twenty drivers picks exactly one winner in twenty, which is arithmetic rather
            than judgement.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A ranking model gave the actual winner zero</dt>
          <dd>
            It scored five times worse than a random guess, assigning a near-certainty to one driver
            in most races, because its confidence was tuned on the same races it had learned from.
            Every other measure looked healthy while this happened — it still picked 52% of winners.
            Only the chances it gave showed the problem.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What this cannot predict.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Safety cars and first-lap incidents</dt>
          <dd>
            A large part of what separates the prediction from the result, and no model can see them
            coming.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Weather on the day</dt>
          <dd>
            A dry forecast that turns wet rearranges a race, and a chance published on a Tuesday
            cannot know that.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Reliability</dt>
          <dd>
            Treated as an average rate, which works over a season and says little about one car on
            one afternoon.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Anything not in the data</dt>
          <dd>Team orders, an undisclosed upgrade, a driver racing unwell.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        There is also one input deliberately missing. The <strong>starting grid</strong> is better
        information than qualifying position because it includes penalties, and it is known before
        the race starts — but there is no data feed that would let the model use it for live
        predictions as well as in training. Learning from it but predicting without it would make
        every score on this site look better than it is, so it stays out until that feed exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .contents {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  .sub-h {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
